course: dictionary
lesson: 7498
title: Words for people who help us
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A dictionary tells you what a word means, and a sentence shows you how it is used. Use both to check your idea. «In Dorset, Esme lets herself in at 3:45 and Dad, a postman, is home at 4.» Dad is a postman, and he is at work during the day, so a postman is someone with a daytime job that takes him out.
explain: A postman is a man whose job is to collect and deliver letters and parcels. A woman who does this job is a postwoman. Other people may say «mail carrier» or «postal worker», which can mean anyone who does this job.
explain: Try your meaning in a sentence: «Esme waited by the door for the postman.» If you think of a person who brings letters, the sentence still makes sense, so your meaning fits.
fact: A postman is a man whose job is to collect and deliver letters and parcels.
fact: A woman who does this job is a postwoman.
fact: In the USA, people often say «mailman» or «mail carrier» instead.
fact: «The postman came» is a main clause, and it makes sense on its own.
fact: «When the postman came» has a verb, but it leans on a main clause and cannot stand alone.
fact: «One dog bit a postman», so «all dogs bite postmen» is not fair: one example does not prove a rule about every dog.
exercise: «The postman came early this morning.» What is a postman's job? | Driving a bus to school | Looking after sick animals | Cooking food in a restaurant | *Collecting and delivering letters and parcels
exercise: Esme says a postman sells newspapers in shops. Is she right? | Yes, that is exactly what a postman does | Yes, a postman works in a bakery | *No, a postman collects and delivers letters and parcels | Yes, but only on Sundays
exercise: A woman who does the same job as a postman is called a ... | parcel | letterbox | postman | *postwoman
test: Which part of this sentence can stand alone? «The dog barked when the postman came.» | the postman came | barked when | when the postman came | *The dog barked.
test: «One dog bit a postman, so "all dogs bite postmen".» Is this a fair rule? | Yes, because postmen are always bitten | No, because dogs never bite | Yes, because the dog bit someone | *No, one example does not prove a rule about all dogs
exam: «In Dorset, Esme lets herself in at 3:45 and Dad, a postman, is home at 4.» What does postman mean? | A dog walker in the park | A cook in a café | *A man whose job is to collect and deliver letters and parcels | A teacher at a school
